Fictional EP Tracklist

Track 1: Locusts Unstitch the Tent
- Story & Context: When Father Delvaux hurled a lit magnesium torch into the four-tiered wedding cake at the Baraboo fairgrounds, the sudden flash illuminated twenty-two masked acrobats frozen mid-air along the center rigging. South Side engineer Cassian Vance cranked the master gain on his modified TR-707 just as the burning canvas roof gave way with the sour stench of singed horsehair and scorched sugar. The stuttering four-on-the-floor kick slammed through the stampede while the runaway bride screamed into a distortion-crusted microphone, shouting: "Tell the preacher that we dance on the fall." What began as an all-out melee between rival lion tamers and wire-walkers instantly locked into a frantic, dust-churning footwork circle that kept the collapsing center pole upright through pure acoustic force.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Macro artwork of a Victorian canvas tent seam bursting open to reveal swarms of metallic, shimmering locusts chewing through heavy velvet rope, backlit by an orange fire glow against a pitch-black midnight sky, cinematic chiaroscuro, surreal album art.

Track 2: "Who Told You the Wire Was Greased?"
- Story & Context: Hiding in the drafty tack room behind the horse stalls, Nadia Orlov scrubbed fresh white chalk and spilled peach schnapps from her trembling collarbones while the bass throbbed through the floorboards. She tearfully confessed to Cassian that she had deliberately swabbed tallow across her own brother's trapeze bar before the opening bridal waltz, desperate to shatter the family's forced dynastic union once and for all. Her voice was looped directly into an Akai sampler as the skipping 2-step garage rhythm stumbled like an off-balance dancer, repeating her whispered confession: "You took my feathers, so I took your ground." Outside the locked doors, the Delvaux groomsmen stalked the perimeter armed with iron stakes, wholly oblivious that their escaped target was singing lead over the very anthem that kept them transfixed.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: A close-up of chalk-caked hands grasping an oily, suspended steel tightrope high above an empty sawdust arena, dramatic low-angle shot, single cold white spotlight cutting through thick theatrical haze, moody and minimalist.

Track 3: The Baraboo Ecdysis
- Story & Context: By daybreak, both circus caravans had completely vanished into the Black-Oak Marsh, leaving behind forty acres of trampled mud, broken violins, and thousands of dead cecropia moths sucked into the cooling vents of abandoned synthesizers. Search parties from Sauk County found the master sequencer still humming off a sputtering diesel generator, automatically firing off shuffling ghost-step percussion to an audience of empty wooden bleachers. A pitch-shifted diva vocal sailed across the damp morning swamp like a lingering curse: "Strip the skin and shake the wire." To this day, local teenagers trespass onto the rusted fairgrounds every autumn equinox to shuffle across the rotted sawdust floor, swearing the ground itself still jacks on the fourth beat.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: An abandoned, rain-soaked circus ring at dawn where a massive, translucent moth cocoon is woven around a towering stacks of vintage synthesizer racks and horn speakers, dewy spiderwebs, grey mist, muted earth tones, haunting atmospheric landscape.

